verification.reasoning:
I reproduced the gap, so the claim holds. declaration_uses (tests/test_seed_registry.py:150-185) reads seeds from only three places: a dict holding `world_seeds_from` (line 157), keys ending in `world_seeds` (line 167), and resource-train/resource-demo command strings that contain --world-seed. Any other key is walked for strings and nothing else. An integer `first_world_seed`, a `seeds` list or a `world_seed_range` string gives no Use and no problem. The file still has to be listed in an experiment's sources (ownership), but coverage, overlap and held-out checks have nothing to check, so all_problems() passes.

Two caveats lower the impact but do not make the claim false:
(1) The reviewer's point about "run from hand commands" is wrong. When a declaration lists its hand commands with --world-seed, they are parsed and the overlap and held-out reuse fail loudly (my P6). The real gap is a declaration that states its seeds under other key names and has no committed plan, no commands carrying --world-seed and no retained runs yet. That is exactly the pre-registration window the registry exists to guard.
(2) Design section 7.5 asks only for every configs/*.json plan and every retained metadata.json to be expanded. It does not require declarations to be read. Plans (`jobs`, first_world_seed) are expanded fully and cross-checked against parse_plan, and the stone experiment's own plan (configs/stone_pickaxe_plan.json, design 8.6) will be caught.

Still, the S1 deliverable promises more than it checks. The registry description says the test "expands every configs/*.json plan and declaration". The test module docstring says every seed "a plan, a declaration or a retained run uses is registered". And the registry tells authors to register a new declaration's seeds before committing it. An author who writes `first_world_seed`, the plans' own key, gets a silent pass instead of the check they were promised.

Severity stays low because:
- both existing declarations use the parsed keys;
- the mandatory stone plan is parsed;
- retained metadata catches actual use after the fact;
- nothing here affects the wood stage or the bot's safety.

